FULL RECORD ssl-v2-reference-warrant.md
# Nondeictic physical naming: specification-learning round

This is a new measured phase under `gse.ssl-construction/2`, following the
singleton-only controller diagnosis. Its records belong beside the existing
lexicon experiment records; it is not a second construction design.

The founder's approved boundary is first-/second-person reference only;
generic selfhood does not supply I or YOU. The exact decision is retained in
`023-approved-person-reference-boundary.json`. The native closed-class I/YOU
definitions are positive controls. The approved generic self definition is an
outside-scope negative control, not an empirical audit result.

The candidate mechanism is own-word naming versus discourse deixis. An exact
native noun definition naming a physical non-person entity or kind does not
thereby refer to the current speaker or addressee. Under the approved bank
meanings its own content is orthogonal to I and YOU. Sentence-level reference
binding and any participant described in its gloss remain separate. This is a
positive type/role argument, not a zero supplied by a missing source field.

The initial sufficient scope is the conjunction of noun POS, an attested native
lemma, a witnessed WordNet `physical_entity.n.01` ancestry and witnessed exclusion
from `person.n.01` ancestry. Both ancestry predicates must be established from
the same exact definition's stored native paths. This deliberately reserves
person/address/pronominal definitions. It does not claim that physicality is a
necessary condition for nondeictic naming, or that every noun has zero deixis.
World associations, alias spellings and unrelated senses cannot activate it.

The first Zipf-aware teaching frontier consists of the exact ordinary water,
body and rock senses. Their source definitions must be inspected and their
eligibility established before frequency orders them. Aggregate spelling
frequency is labelled as such; it is not attributed to a rare homograph.
Holdouts span animals, plants, artifacts and matter, use distinct native senses
and spellings, and are disjoint from teaching. The proposed rules and their
answers are withheld from direct audit requests.

Stage each rule separately and together over the entire eligible population.
Retain exact contradictions, model-prefix reconsiderations, partial narrowing
and joint-space contraction. Independent model disagreement is a typed challenge
to inspect, not grounds for inventing an exception or voting a sign into place.
Admission requires the semantic warrant and successful independent audits;
model agreement alone is not semantic gold. If a counterexample changes the
guard, record the narrower mechanism and re-stage the entire scope.

Measure baseline, direct teaching and rule propagation separately, including
the full-lexicon frequency buckets. An accepted model prefix may be archived by
the existing rule migration when its context changes; reopened cells remain
unknown and are reported. Full replay is required before publication. This
round does not claim to complete the lexicon, all foundational laws, general
rule-universe discovery, or the common-language runtime checkpoint.


FULL RECORD SOURCE_SCOPE.md
# Explicit person, place, and time genera

Release 011 provides the baseline. The rule families use complete native noun glosses with exact WordNet or Wiktionary identity. They identify the genus of the defined sense. They leave the type of the modifying action, state, or participant to separately warranted rules. [Prior capacity rule](../2026-11-13-ssl-person-capacity/SOURCE_SCOPE.md) · [Saved NSM chart](../../../data/external/references/nsm-primes-chart-v20-2022.txt)

- The person rule extends the audited coordinated-person scope to a single genus introduced by *a/the person* or *someone* and a subject relative. Negation in the relative can restrict the description while preserving the person genus. Definitions such as *a person who is part mortal and part god* are reserved for review of their mixed ontology. Distinct senses of the same lemma retain separate domains. The held-out senses test a person who receives a vaccine and a person who drives racing cars.
- The place rule covers *a place where* and *a place in which*. The NSM WHERE prime explicitly permits the place of an event. The location can be defined by what is kept there, what happens there, or who meets there. The scope reserves the definite form for a later review of conventional, imagined, and metaphorical locations. The held-out senses test a place where bees are kept and a place where people meet.
- The time rule covers *a/the time* and *a/the period of time* followed by *when* or *during which*. Both points and intervals can identify a WHEN coordinate. The rule leaves extent, duration attachment, and the referenced event to their own evidence. The held-out senses test the onset of daylight and the interval in which a contract is effective.

The experiment forecasts 355 SOMEONE, 68 WHERE, and 35 WHEN assignments. On the exact Wiktionary *aliterate* sense, the person rule also supplies the missing premise of the admitted CAN rule. That interaction adds one CAN assignment to the joint forecast. The release checks reserve competing genera and homographs, and they distinguish source records from distinct native sense groups.

## Measured replay

Three typed teaching questions and six held-out audits supported the joint revision. It resolved 459 cells, with 456 changes beyond the teaching cells, and removed 727.50 bits of marginal potential, or 0.00207857% of the residual on 011. All 401,596 records replayed with zero mismatches and no reopenings. Removing the newly admitted person rule reopened CAN on the Wiktionary *aliterate* sense. [Audit and replay](verification.json) · [Frozen scope and exact changes](population-stage.json)

| Bank | Wiktionary | WordNet |
|---|---:|---:|
| SOMEONE | 342 | 13 |
| WHERE | 48 | 20 |
| WHEN | 19 | 16 |
| CAN | 1 | 0 |

## Explicit stative content

The next bank proposal applies the approved BE convention requiring the definition's own stative content. Its exact native noun gloss must begin with *a/the state of* or *a/the state in which*, and its verified WordNet ancestry must contain `state.n.02`. Both the surface construction and the type are required. Political or geographical states, a person in a state, and entries with alternative defining genera are outside this construction. The generic state ancestry alone includes broader definitions such as the course-of-life sense of *life*, which also defines actions and events. [BE convention](../../program/LEXICON_CONSTRUCTION_DESIGN.md#be-stative-content-scope)

The first unadmitted census found 414 applicable definitions representing 227 distinct glosses. Reviewing every gloss located seven records under `nonbeing.n.01`, including the states of not being, not existing, and nonexistence. A separate polarity decision is required for that branch, so the child rule excludes the verified branch. Negative predicates such as *not being ripe* remain stative descriptions of a condition. This distinction follows exact ancestry and the complete definition. [Parent preview](state-preview-016.json)

The scoped proposal uses the already established SOMETHING=+1 as a premise for BE=+1. Its warrant is the state denoted by the noun itself. It supplies no conclusion about whether its participants are alive, bodily, or wanting. The curriculum orders eligible senses by SUBTLEX spelling count and reserves *absence*, *balance*, and the state-of-change sense of *motion* for direct audits. Those definitions test absence, equilibrium, and a dynamic state's compatibility with the proposed stative component. *Arrest* as inactivity, *sleeping* as being asleep, and *anger* as being angry are separate release controls.

`stative.py` prepares and runs one epoch through the existing persistent Zipf controller after the Wiktionary transfer release. Rule admission still requires source review, disjoint typed audits, a complete scope forecast, exact delta comparison, and full replay. The state previews are unadmitted forecasts.

The typed exclusion reserves ten matching records across the full nonbeing branch. The resulting child preview projected 404 BE assignments and zero contradictions on release 016. Its selected teaching sense was *want* as extreme poverty. That preview required restaging against its actual successor baseline. [Refined preview](state-refined-preview-016.json)

## Stative epoch on release 017

Restaging on the validated active 017 release retained 404 eligible cells. Zipf selected `wordnet:want:privation.n.01`, defined as a state of extreme poverty, at a SUBTLEX spelling count of 140,718. The typed writer accepted BE=+1. Staging then found 403 further singleton assignments and zero contradictions. The withheld definitions of *absence*, *balance*, and *motion* each returned BE=+1 with their BE target open and SOMETHING=+1 established independently. These four typed evaluations support the proposed scope; full migration and replay remain required before publication. [Pinned plan](../../../data/lexicon_builder/2026-11-12/ssl-explicit-genera/stative-018/plan.json) · [Audit contracts](018-audit-contract-check.json)

A five-case runtime diagnostic was frozen on 017 before rule admission. It covers the state senses of *arrest*, *sleeping*, and *anger*, and reserves BE on *nonbeing* and *nonexistence*. All five sentences abstained at sense selection on the baseline. The three positive BE targets were open on their exact definitions; both reserved targets were also open. The publication path compiles the three positive definitions through `GSEEncoder.encode_definition`, checks the reserved domains, reruns these sentence diagnostics, and rejects regression or any BE emission on a reserved unknown. It reports lexical coverage separately from contextual sense selection. [Diagnostic cases](018-stative-diagnostic-cases.json) · [Baseline](018-stative-diagnostic-baseline.json)

## Verified stative replay and release 018

The controller admitted the rule after the three withheld audits passed. The exact full-corpus comparison found 404 BE domains moving from `[-1, 0, 1]` to `[1]`, including the teaching cell, with no reopenings or other domain changes. The frozen scope contains 400 lemmas and 222 controller native sense groups. Four typed evaluations supported 403 assignments beyond the teaching cell, for 101 new cells per typed question within this scope and 640.32 bits of marginal-domain contraction. Both the migration replay and isolated publication replay validated all 401,596 records with complete search and zero mismatches. [Contraction and denominators](018-contraction.json) · [Controller and replay](018-verification.json)

The existing sentence and ambiguity benchmarks reproduced without regression. All three positive state definitions compiled successfully. In the added five-case diagnostic, the three positive exact-definition cells became correct, while both reserved domains remained unknown and emitted no BE sign. All five sentences continued to abstain at sense selection. This diagnostic records word-level coverage and a remaining abstention in this single-sentence entry point. [Candidate diagnostic](018-stative-diagnostic-candidate.json)

